{% comment %}
  Overrides Horizon's final type variables with Mionas design-system tokens from
  assets/design-tokens.css.

  Rendered AFTER theme-styles-variables so these declarations win the cascade.

  Only size, line-height and letter-spacing are bridged: they are the only three values
  Shopify settings cannot express (the size enum has no 38/28/22, line-heights are ratios,
  and tracking is capped at +/-0.03em where the brand needs 0.02em). Family, weight and case
  come from the theme's font settings so Shopify keeps hosting and preloading the faces.

  Token names are mirrored in scripts/design-tokens-contract.mjs, which fails the sync if any
  of them stop existing.
{% endcomment %}
{%- comment -%}
  Archivo 500 (medium). The theme's four font settings load Archivo at 400 (body), 600
  (subheading) and 800 (heading), each plus its font_modify'd bold -- so the browser has
  400/600/700/800 and no 500. The design system loads 400;500;600;700;800 (../design-system/
  src/fonts.css), and several of its tokens are 500: --link-base-font-weight,
  --button-base-font-weight, and the caption/label text roles.

  CSS font matching for a requested 500 with no 500 face checks 500, then descends -- landing on
  400. So every one of those tokens silently rendered at body weight, with no error anywhere:
  the token exists, the contract passes, the var() resolves, and the text is just wrong. That is
  what made links indistinguishable from body copy.

  There is no fifth Shopify font setting to hang this on, so derive the face from the body font
  and emit it here. Guarded because font_modify returns blank when a weight isn't in Shopify's
  library for that family, and font_face on blank emits nothing -- exactly the silent fallback
  this is fixing. If the guard ever goes false, 500 text drops back to 400 and this comment is
  the place to start.

  Preloaded as well as declared, mirroring what snippets/fonts.liquid does for the four settings
  fonts. The @font-face alone is what makes the weight render -- a preload never was load-bearing
  for correctness -- but without it this face is the only Archivo weight the browser discovers
  late, so 500 text flashes the fallback under font-display: swap while every other weight is
  already warm. It also means n5 shows up alongside n4/n6/n8 in the head, so "is the medium face
  there?" stays answerable by reading the markup, which is where anyone will look first.

  Lives here rather than in snippets/fonts.liquid because that file is stock Horizon and this
  theme merges from Shopify/horizon -- keeping all the Archivo-500 handling in this one
  Mionas-owned snippet keeps it out of the way of upstream merges.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign body_font_medium = settings.type_body_font | font_modify: 'weight', '500' -%}
{%- if body_font_medium -%}
  {{ body_font_medium | font_url | preload_tag: as: 'font', type: 'font/woff2', fetchpriority: 'low' }}
  {% style %}
    {{ body_font_medium | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
  {% endstyle %}
{%- endif -%}
{% style %}
  :root {
    /* Font families: the design system's two primitives, repointed at the faces this theme
       actually loads. A --font-family-* token is an inert string -- a custom property loads no
       font file -- so spending one renders Archivo only by coincidence, and falls back the
       moment a font setting changes. Shopify hosts and preloads fonts through theme settings, so
       --font-body--family / --font-accent--family are the only families that are really here.

       This is the whole font-family story for the theme. Every component token
       (--link-base-font-family, --card-base-font-family, ...) is a var() reference to one of
       these two in dist/tokens.flat.css, so blocks spend their own component token, mirror their
       .module.css verbatim, and resolve through here automatically. A new design-system component
       needs no change in this file. Both names are pinned by value in
       scripts/design-tokens-contract.mjs, so re-pointing one design-system side fails the sync
       instead of silently keeping the mapping below. */
    --font-family-archivo: var(--font-body--family);
    --font-family-oswald: var(--font-accent--family);

    /* h1 -> hero. Fixed, not clamped, regardless of the token's value -- this overrides
       --font-h1--size directly (base.css:704 reads it as a plain property), bypassing
       theme-styles-variables.liquid's own settings-driven clamp() generation entirely. That
       matters here because `hero` is back at 48px, which sits exactly at Horizon's 48px fluid
       cutoff (the same value that previously drove display-lg through a clamp() with a derived
       2.75rem/44px floor, before the design system collapsed the display family into `hero` at
       38px). Were this bridged through Shopify's own type_size_h1 setting instead of a direct
       token override, landing on the cutoff would reintroduce that clamp() -- so keep this a
       direct var() reference, not a value Shopify's fluid-size logic ever sees. */
    --font-h1--size: var(--text-role-hero-font-size);
    --font-h1--line-height: var(--text-role-hero-line-height);
    --font-h1--letter-spacing: var(--text-role-hero-letter-spacing);

    /* h2 -> title. Below the fluid cutoff, so fixed. */
    --font-h2--size: var(--text-role-title-font-size);
    --font-h2--line-height: var(--text-role-title-line-height);
    --font-h2--letter-spacing: var(--text-role-title-letter-spacing);

    /* h3 -> title. Same role as h2: the collapsed scale has one 28px step. */
    --font-h3--size: var(--text-role-title-font-size);
    --font-h3--line-height: var(--text-role-title-line-height);
    --font-h3--letter-spacing: var(--text-role-title-letter-spacing);

    /* h4 -> heading */
    --font-h4--size: var(--text-role-heading-font-size);
    --font-h4--line-height: var(--text-role-heading-line-height);
    --font-h4--letter-spacing: var(--text-role-heading-letter-spacing);

    /* h5 -> subheading */
    --font-h5--size: var(--text-role-subheading-font-size);
    --font-h5--line-height: var(--text-role-subheading-line-height);
    --font-h5--letter-spacing: var(--text-role-subheading-letter-spacing);

    /* h6 -> caption. The design system's ladder now gives h6 its own step below subheading
       (15px vs 18px) rather than sharing h5's size. */
    --font-h6--size: var(--text-role-caption-font-size);
    --font-h6--line-height: var(--text-role-caption-line-height);
    --font-h6--letter-spacing: var(--text-role-caption-letter-spacing);

    /* paragraph -> body */
    --font-paragraph--size: var(--text-role-body-font-size);
    --font-paragraph--line-height: var(--text-role-body-line-height);
    --font-paragraph--letter-spacing: var(--text-role-body-letter-spacing);

    /* Buttons — Horizon states no height: it derives one from padding-block plus the paragraph
       line-height (base.css:1255). The design system states height directly (48px) with 24px
       inline padding. Derive the padding-block that yields the design system's height at the
       line-height this bridge already set: (48px - 24px) / 2 = 12px. */
    --button-padding-block: calc(
      (var(--button-size-md-height) - var(--text-role-body-line-height)) / 2
    );
    --button-padding-inline: var(--button-size-md-padding);
  }

  /* Font smoothing. The design system sets none anywhere, so its components rasterise with the
     platform default -- subpixel antialiasing on macOS. Horizon sets
     -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased in five places, and the first is `body` (base.css:677).
     The property inherits, so that one declaration reaches every element on the page, the Mionas
     mirrors included.

     Grayscale antialiasing renders visibly lighter than subpixel at an identical weight, which is
     why the whole theme still read thinner than Storybook after the Archivo 500 face was loading
     correctly. Two different causes, the same symptom; this is the second one.

     The universal selector and the !important are both deliberate. Four of the five declarations
     live in files this theme merges from Shopify/horizon (base.css, overflow-list.css,
     header-actions.liquid, contact-form.liquid) and their selectors range from `body` to
     `slot[name='more'] .button`. Restating that list here would mean re-copying upstream selectors
     after every merge, and any declaration Shopify adds later would silently restore the thin
     rendering. Normalising one inherited property once is the version that keeps working.
     blocks/corporate-quote-form.liquid is the fifth; it is this theme's own file, so the
     declaration was removed there outright rather than overridden here. */
  *,
  *::before,
  *::after {
    -webkit-font-smoothing: auto !important;
  }

  /* Horizon gives button labels the paragraph weight (400, base.css:1245). No Shopify setting
     exposes button weight, so this needs a rule.

     Spends the token rather than a hardcoded step, so native Horizon buttons and
     blocks/mionas-button.liquid stay at one weight. This was --font-weight-semibold (600) until
     ../design-system bd2ec5e retokenized Button.module.css onto --button-base-font-weight (500);
     the commit message doesn't mention weight, so the drop was confirmed deliberate with the
     design system's author rather than inferred from the diff. Note this is one of the weights
     that only renders correctly because of the Archivo 500 face emitted above.

     Same selector list as base.css:1229-1232, and this snippet renders after base.css, so it
     wins on source order at equal specificity.
     .button-unstyled is excluded: it strips padding/background/border to read as plain text
     (base.css:1361), and must not be weighted up. base.css uses this same :not() idiom at
     line 1265. */
  .button:not(.button-unstyled),
  .button-secondary,
  .button-custom,
  button.shopify-payment-button__button--unbranded {
    font-weight: var(--button-base-font-weight);
  }

  /* Footer tagline — a merchant-added `text` block inside the footer group, set to
     type_preset "custom" with font "Accent" (Oswald), reads as the design system's tagline line
     (e.g. "Bakery & specialty coffee · Sitges"). `text.liquid`/`typography-style.liquid` always
     emit `--font-family: var(--font-accent--family);` inline plus a `custom-typography` class for
     that combination, so this targets it directly rather than adding a dedicated Horizon block
     type. Scoped to the real `<footer>` element `theme.liquid` wraps the footer-group render in,
     so it can't affect accent-font custom text elsewhere on the page. */
  footer .custom-typography[style*='--font-family: var(--font-accent--family)'] {
    color: var(--footer-tagline-foreground);
  }
{% endstyle %}

{%- comment -%}
  Reskin of Horizon's native product card. It opens its own style block, and rendering it from here
  rather than from layout/theme.liquid means adding it touched no native Horizon file at all, so
  merges from upstream Shopify/horizon stay clean. This snippet already renders after
  theme-styles-variables and after base.css, which is the cascade position the bridge needs -- the
  same one header-bridge.liquid gets from its own render in theme.liquid.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% render 'product-card-bridge' %}
